{"agent":"impact","findings":[{"id":"impact-1","file":"triggarr/web/routes.py","line":1578,"title":"tempfile.mkstemp sits outside the try block, so an unwritable config dir returns HTTP 500 and breaks the D-17 non-fatal guarantee","category":"blast-radius","cwe":"CWE-755","severity":"medium","agent_confidence":80,"in_diff":true,"intent_doc_match":"contradicts D-17 (file write failure is non-fatal) as stated in the function's own docstring","problem":"`fd, tmp_path = tempfile.mkstemp(dir=path.parent, suffix=".tmp")` runs before the `try:`. If the config volume is read-only (EROFS), owned by another UID (EACCES) or out of inodes or space (ENOSPC), mkstemp raises OSError. The `except OSError` handler never sees it. The exception travels through `run_in_executor` into `reset_request_post`, which has no handler, so the operator gets a 500 instead of the neutral confirmation. By then the token is already minted (line ~1680) and logged, so recovery still works through docker logs. But the user sees an error page, and the docstring's promise ('Do NOT re-raise', 'file write is non-fatal') does not hold for the most likely failure, an unwritable directory. Separately, if `os.fchmod(fd, ...)` raises, the raw fd is never closed because `os.fdopen` has not wrapped it yet. The except branch unlinks tmp_path but leaks the descriptor.","current_code":"fd, tmp_path = tempfile.mkstemp(dir=path.parent, suffix=".tmp")
    try:
        os.fchmod(fd, 0o600)
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as f:","fix_hint":"Move mkstemp inside the try (with tmp_path initialized to None and the unlink guarded), and close the raw fd in the except path when fdopen has not taken ownership of it.","why_it_matters":"Docker deployments often mount the config volume with mismatched UIDs or read-only. In those cases the documented recovery flow shows a 500 page, which tells the operator recovery failed even though the token is in the logs.","silenced_marker_nearby":false},{"id":"impact-2","file":"triggarr/web/routes.py","line":1650,"title":"Unauthenticated /reset/request waits on the shared search_lock, which a scheduler search cycle holds for its full duration, so unauthenticated waiters pile up","category":"perf-at-scale","cwe":"CWE-400","severity":"low","agent_confidence":55,"in_diff":true,"intent_doc_match":"mirrors search_now (D-15), but search_now is an authenticated route","problem":"The scheduler `job()` holds `app.state.search_lock` for an entire `_run_one_cycle`, which makes network calls to Radarr/Sonarr (triggarr/search/scheduler.py:131-165). The optimistic rate-limit check runs outside the lock, and `last_reset_time` is only stamped inside the lock (line ~1656). So while a cycle holds the lock, every unauthenticated POST /reset/request passes the optimistic check and queues on the lock. That means an unbounded number of pending coroutines and open connections, each served in FIFO order ahead of later authenticated mutations (settings saves, search_now, auth changes) that use the same lock. Each waiter holds the lock only briefly once it gets it, and most return 429. The cost is latency and connection buildup from unauthenticated traffic rather than a lockout. pending: measure typical cycle length on a large library and confirm the reverse proxy concurrency limits.","current_code":"async with request.app.state.search_lock:
        # Re-check inside lock to prevent concurrent bypass (D-15, mirrors DRSEC-03)","fix_hint":"Use a dedicated asyncio.Lock for reset state; the reset path does not touch any search-guarded state.","why_it_matters":"This is the first unauthenticated route that acquires the search lock, so anonymous clients can now delay authenticated mutations behind a long-running search cycle.","silenced_marker_nearby":false},{"id":"impact-3","file":"triggarr/web/routes.py","line":1688,"title":"Reset token also lands unredacted in the in-app log buffer shown in the web UI, not only in docker logs","category":"blast-radius","cwe":"CWE-532","severity":"low","agent_confidence":40,"in_diff":true,"intent_doc_match":"comment claims the token goes to 'operator-only sinks' (docker logs and the 0600 file)","problem":"`setup_logging` also registers `buffer_sink` (triggarr/logging.py:72-84). It redacts only `collect_secrets` values, and the reset token is deliberately left out of that list. So the warning line with the token goes into `log_buffer`, which the dashboard and log viewer render (routes.py:382, 1083). That is a third sink nobody intended, reachable by anyone who can view the dashboard. In Forms/Basic modes that person is already authenticated, so the exposure adds little. pending: show a deployment where the dashboard log view is reachable by someone who should not be able to reset the password (for example an auth method that leaves the dashboard open while reset still applies).","current_code":"logger.warning(
            "Password reset token minted. Read from 'docker logs' or reset-token.txt: {token}",
            token=token,
        )","fix_hint":"Bind an extra flag (e.g. logger.bind(no_buffer=True)) and have buffer_sink skip records carrying it.","why_it_matters":"The design says the token appears only in operator-only channels. The web log viewer is a UI-visible channel the design did not account for.","silenced_marker_nearby":false}],"agent_notes":["Blast radius: the change only adds code. It adds two new routes (GET/POST /reset/request), a private helper, and an import of `generate_reset_token` (exists at triggarr/auth.py:70). No existing public signature changes, and none of routes.py's 16 importers are affected. No DB schema or migration impact; state is in-memory (`app.state.reset_token`, `app.state.last_reset_time`, both initialized at triggarr/search/scheduler.py:504-507).","Dependencies verified: RESET_REQUEST_RATE_LIMIT_SECONDS defined at routes.py:146, `asyncio` imported at routes.py:10, `_runtime_config_dir` at routes.py:97. The middleware exempts /reset/* through an exact-or-'/reset/' predicate (triggarr/web/middleware.py:118), so the new routes are reachable without authentication, as intended.","Rate limiting uses one global key ('request'). An anonymous client can make the operator's own request return 429 for up to 60s, but the attacker's request mints a token into the operator's logs anyway, and the H1 live-token guard prevents supersession. Net effect is harmless.","reset-token.txt is never deleted or rewritten after the 900s expiry, so an expired token stays on disk until the next mint. Whether the confirm path deletes the file on use is outside this diff. Worth checking in the confirm-step plan.","Expiry uses time.monotonic, so a token does not survive a restart. That matches 'in-memory token is the authority' but leaves a stale file after a restart. Expected behavior; operators may be confused if the file shows a token that no longer works.","Verdict: shippable after fixing the mkstemp placement (impact-1). The other two items are hardening."]}

